   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022-23, the Jordan Chamber of Industry (JCI) upgraded its service offer in the areas of employment and productivity. To expand its services on employment, JCI established a new Labour market unit composed of a labour economist, a labour lawyer and two skills specialists in October 2022. JCI redefined the mandate of the unit, and launched several different services. For example, information on how to comply with labour law and social security regulations including videos, infographics, and PPT are available on JCI social media and website. JCI also established a HR Managers&#x2019; network and organized meetings on labour related matters. Since January 2023, JCI held 4 meetings covering topics on maternity leave, termination, social security benefits, and skills shortages. Additionally, JCI set up a dedicated line for queries on labour related matters. In 8 months, around 200 queries were received and advice provided. Most queries related to workers termination, social security, and a new employment programme with incentives for firms hiring new people. According to JCI management, member companies appreciate the new service and it has potential to expand further.

On productivity enhancement, JCI developed a new methodology for a rapid productivity assessment focussed on measuring working conditions and four productivity levers: sales revenue, output per unit cost of production, labour utilization and capital utilization. JCI piloted the methodology in 30 SMEs and supported the implementation of productivity enhancement plans in 15 SMEs. The methodology meets the needs of Jordan&#x2019;s key industrial sectors and is the basis for a new company service with the potential for becoming a paid JCI service. Additionally, JCI adopted the ILO SCORE methodology as an advanced productivity assessment service, and trained 14 trainers capable of performing SCORE assessments.
 ILO provided technical expertise and support in following areas:
1. Conducting a rapid market assessment on labour law-related services provided in the country by chambers and law firms. The market study was instrumental to identify a market niche for JCI. 
2. Forming (redefining mandate, areas of operations, etc.) of the new labour market unit.
3. Providing capacity building and coaching for JCI staff.
4. Overseeing the development of set of training and information materials targeting SMEs on how to comply with labour legislation.

The ILO also supported JCI to develop a solid working method for the new labour market unit, and assisted aspects related to quality control on the advisory, training and information services.

With the support of PROSPECTS project, the ILO assisted JCI design a new methodology for a new productivity assessment service. The methodology was tested in SMEs in Jordan and selected SMEs received co-funding provided in the framework of the project for the implementation of the agreed productivity enhancement plan. PROSPECTS project co-funded (up to 50%) the implementation of participating SMEs&#x2019; productivity enhancement plans - totalling around US$250,000. Equally, companies contributed themselves around US$ 250,000. 

Additionally, with PROSPECTS support, selected staff of JCI participated in a Training the Trainers workshop on ILO SCORE methodology and became the first ILO SCORE certified trainers in Jordan. The ILO supported the certified JCI trainers test the SCORE in 15 companies. Moving beyond the pilot, JCI will explore the expansion of the programme in selected industrial sectors in Jordan.</narrative>
   </description>
